+
+## Editorial findings
+
+### 1. Release blocker: protected-text placeholders remain in the prose
+
+Ten raw Tier-B notes occur at chapter 49 lines 99 and 169; chapter 50 lines 113, 173, 177, and 245; chapter 51 lines 21 and 323; chapter 52 line 265; and chapter 53 line 291. They replace, respectively, “They knew”; the “Nobody declares…” exchange; “Through”; “Nothing was gained inside”; “By count”; “You'll die in there” / “We've been in three times”; the three-line arm warning; the bearing “Through”; “The Compact … Yes. Part of it”; and the S20 closing exchange.
+
+This is not cosmetic markup. The notes expose planning logic, offer alternative speakers, and tell a coordinator what to write. They interrupt the central fight, the injury aftermath, the Compact disclosure, and the movement's closing hook. Because several units are protected wording, ordinary paraphrase is not an acceptable repair. Speaker assignment and minimal staging must respect the sequence locks already cited in the notes: the tall man and older healer remain silent to the six; Oryn owns “Everybody says that now”; and the S20 alternation begins with Karis.
+
+### 2. The evidence sequence is intellectually strong but audio-dense
+
+Chapter 52, especially lines 117–221, asks the reader to track several physical documents and several meanings of “page” at once. The logical chain is sound: the old year is narrowed, the lamp behavior is separated from its cause, Karis moves her measured leaf away from the folded testimony, and Cael keeps a third page off the blanket. On paper, the spatial metaphor rewards attention. For a thirteen-year-old listening, “the hide,” “the clean sheet,” “six sheets,” “the notebook,” “the ruled leaf,” “a folded paper,” “two pages,” and “a third page” can momentarily blur object, document, and category.
+
+The answer is not to simplify the discovery or collapse the two pages; their separation is the moral and epistemic center of the chapter. A very small orientation pass—reasserting which physical object sits at which end of the blanket at one or two transitions, and ensuring pronouns have unmistakable antecedents—would preserve the reasoning while lowering working-memory load.
+
+### 3. Story and character architecture are working
+
+The movement has a clear escalation: road competence, a site reading, reconnaissance, a fight solved by width, an evidentiary discovery, and a controlled approach to the next danger. Teague's field knowledge becomes Cael's bodily lesson; Karis's discipline stops inference from becoming fact; Vastin's history becomes useful without making him the solver; and the young one earns attachment through deeds. The best choice is Cael's refusal to test Force in chapter 53. It makes progression costly and ethical, preserves Teague's knowledge boundary, and converts a new ability into character rather than fireworks.
+
+## Canon and continuity audit
+
+No verified canon contradiction was found against the supplied `BOOK_MAP`, `STATE_LEDGER` through movement 7, `CANON_RULES`, `UNIVERSE_BIBLE`, and movement plan. The following checks passed:
+
+- **Calendar and resources:** days 299–305 progress in order; Vastin moves from day 20 to day 26; Oryn's cairn passage begins on day 300 and advances consistently; the purse drops from 127 to 123 and then holds.
+- **Progression state:** the seep raises confirmed deployment from 32 to 33. The Span yields the thirteenth acquired fragment, Force-adjacent, but chapter 53's dry alignment does not fire it and therefore does not add a deployment.
+- **Power and knowledge limits:** Teague is not told that Cael acquired his capability. Cael refuses activation near camp and before consulting the healer. The tall man does not speak to the six; the older healer remains silent; the broad man's “Aye” at chapter 53 line 33 is his first word to the six.
+- **Bodies and consent:** Teague's arm and the broad man's leg carry real cost beyond the fight. Oryn asks permission before touching the shin at chapter 53 lines 41–47. Violence remains consequential and non-gory.
+- **Reserved disclosures:** Karis states only what the measurements support. The two pages remain separate, the seals remain unnamed, and the prose does not introduce the forbidden terminal/circuit/infrastructure explanation or disclose the reserved cause early.
+- **Documents and objects:** the copied signs, fixed log, tapes, directive, objection, and separate leaves remain distinct. Seln's case stays shut and its contents stay unnamed.
+
+The universe material's legacy description of Rune as “partial Bronze” is superseded here by the protected notice's “Tier equivalent unknown,” as directed by `BOOK_MAP` conflict ruling C5; the manuscript follows that ruling.
+
+## Protected-wording audit
+
+I checked every protected unit in `BOOK_MAP` that falls inside this movement.
+
+Rendered wording is intact in the thirteenth-fragment notice (chapter 50 line 129), the fixed win sentence (50:301), the S19 log (51:339), the lamp finding (52:181), the ruler line (52:197), the directive (52:239), the objection (52:249), the crack line (52:261), the broad man's first “Aye” (53:33), and the final sentence (53:293). No substitution drift was found in those rendered units.
+
+The ten other applicable Tier-B units have not been rendered at all; their placeholders are listed in Finding 1. The protected final sentence, “He was right. He did not yet know how right,” is present, but the protected S20 exchange immediately before it is still a note, so the closer is not yet complete.
+
+## Reader Standard
+
+The movement passes the content standard for a thirteen-year-old: language is clean; there is no sexual content or innuendo; the fighting avoids gore; harm has aftermath; and consent is explicit before healing. Its moral center is unusually clear without preaching: nobody is left under the barricade, the defeated watch is not pursued, water is shared, an enemy's head is kept off stone, Karis refuses to turn inference into fact, and Cael refuses an unsafe secret test.
+
+Vocabulary is highly accessible, but accessibility scores do not remove the chapter 52 listening burden or the role-name load (“broad man,” “tall man,” “young one,” two healers) during fast action. Those design choices remain intelligible because behavior differentiates the cast; the limited orientation repair proposed above is sufficient. The raw coordinator notes are the only absolute Reader Standard failure because they are not story prose.
+
+## Source reuse
+
+Using the supplied `source_overlap.py` result, there are **0 unprotected shared runs of eight or more words** and **18 protected runs**, which are allowed. There is no source-reuse finding.
+
+## Numerical formula alignment
+
+### Coverage and method
+
+The supplied measurement covers all seven chapters: 38,009 prose words, 2,701 sentences, 1,086 written paragraphs, and 32 marked scene breaks. I used the provided output and did not re-estimate it. The method is `editions/monroe-1.3/tools/formula_metrics.py` as described in its module docstring: Markdown headings and marked breaks are stripped for sentence/readability analysis; paragraph units are nonempty Markdown lines; scene breaks are exact marked separators; sentences are split by a punctuation/whitespace/capitalization heuristic with abbreviation protection; and syllables use a vowel-group heuristic with silent-e/-le handling. Proper names and system terms are processed by that same heuristic.
+
+One qualification matters: `words_in_system_notices` is reported as 0 even though ten bracketed editorial placeholders remain in the chapter files. Those notes are therefore included in the reported prose population rather than excluded as notices. The supplied values are the required baseline, but formula compliance is provisional until the protected text is restored and the same tool is rerun.
+
