+Interest caught when Teague began teaching Cael to read the ground in chapter 47, sharpened at the shale-back seep in chapter 48, and peaked in the Span fight and the blanket inquiry. It slipped during some of the route-and-record setup in chapters 47–48, then stopped outright whenever a long `[[TIER-B: ... coordinator to supply protected text]]` note replaced dialogue or narration. The young one matters most beyond Cael: his offered water, frightened crossing, ravine descent, and unasked-for care of the fallen broad man turn him from an anonymous burden into the person whose coming loss will hurt.
+
+I expect the notch/pass ahead to force the first consequential choice about Cael's new Force-adjacent capability and to test whether the wall contract can survive an ambush. I would continue. The field problem, the old line, and the evidence of an internal Compact dispute all open forward pressure; the placeholders must be repaired before this can function as a finished reader experience.
